WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:03.603
Hello, everyone.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:03.937
It is

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:05.071
an honor to join you

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:06.039
in commemorating

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:08.441
the life and legacy of Ed Zigler.

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:12.379
Ed was a mentor, teacher and friend.

00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:15.548
His work has been inspirational to me

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:19.386
and many others in our field, and his profound

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:22.088
influence extends well beyond psychology.

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:23.590
For me,

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.493
he set an example of how science and policy

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:30.230
can join hands to benefit the lives of many.

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:35.802
Ed mobilized science in the service of the public interest.

00:00:46.246 --> 00:00:48.815
I’m a— was a poor child.

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:51.217
That's why I became so interested

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:53.520
in the Head Start program.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.923
I actually had my own Head Start program.

00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:59.325
My parents

00:00:59.526 --> 00:01:01.261
were immigrants.

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:03.797
English was not my first language.

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:05.665
It was back in those days,

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:06.866
and to this day,

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:08.435
there's a phenomenon in this country

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:10.136
that we call settlement houses,

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:11.771
 like the Henry Settlement House

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:13.740
in New York City.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.775
And these settlement houses

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.310
were actually terrific.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.312
They helped enculturate

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:21.414
these families into the country.

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:23.516
Ed talked about his childhood

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:24.517
in Kansas City,

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:25.885
and he talked about being

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:28.421
the child of immigrants and being poor

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:29.856
and growing up

00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:31.624
really in a rough and tumble world,

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:34.961
and how that prepared him for his work

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:36.863
and really inspired him to do the work

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:38.865
that he did later in life.

00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:40.400
And he also talked about

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.235
what a good street fighter he was

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:43.736
and how that was necessary

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:45.839
in the world in which he grew up in.

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:47.674
We went to Woodland.

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:49.776
It was a demonstration school.

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:51.411
It was hooked to the Kansas City

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:52.946
Teachers College.

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:56.249
Reading

00:01:56.583 --> 00:02:00.653
interest in the community, community services:

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:03.456
all of those kinds of things were part

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:07.227
of our curriculum and were very natural to us.

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:08.728
Ed invited me to have a beer

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:09.963
with one of his sisters,

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:10.864
Maurine Argon,

00:02:10.864 --> 00:02:12.298
who was an interior decorator

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:14.100
living in the Kansas City area.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.602
They called him Eddie.

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:19.172
It was a reminder that Ed was a human being,

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:23.243
in addition to being a titan of early childhood education.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.712
I don't think my dad would have accomplished

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:27.981
half of what he accomplished,

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:29.449
if it were not for my mom.

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:32.152
She was a remarkable, smart, funny woman

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:34.420
who had to have the patience of Job

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.523
to be married to my dad.

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:38.958
I used to play, you know,

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:41.261
no-limit poker when I was a young man.

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:46.566
I quit college once to go to New Orleans

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:49.836
and become a professional poker player.

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:52.172
I didn't last down

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:54.507
there long either, but

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.711
the game we had in the department,

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:01.147
I beat these guys for like 30 years.

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:03.483
He taught me to gamble, very young.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:04.951
Taught me how to gamble, play poker,

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:05.885
play blackjack, shoot craps, 

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.854
bet on sports.

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:09.589
When Ed served in Korea,

00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:11.858
he won enough money to buy his parents

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:13.293
their first home.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:15.094
He won all the time there,

00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:17.297
so he stopped playing poker.

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:18.631
The challenge was gone.

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:19.866
Plus, he felt guilty

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:21.534
taking money from his comrades,

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:23.403
payday after payday.

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:25.038
He broke his fast just once, 

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:26.839
just once, shortly before discharge.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.141
He played the camp's

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:29.576
pharmacist with the stakes

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:31.077
being the seasickness pills

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:33.446
he needed for the long voyage home.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.014
He won.

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:38.251
But yea, I'm a very good poker player.

00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:39.819
Taught my son when I was about

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:41.955
four.

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:45.091
He's a good poker player now.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:46.993
He did give us all a strong sense

00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:49.128
of the importance of social justice

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:51.331
and the importance of

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:51.898
taking care

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:53.933
of those people in society who society

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:55.835
otherwise seemed to want to overlook.

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:56.903
He loved poker,

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.371
but I think in many ways

00:03:58.371 --> 00:03:59.639
he was a poker player in

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:00.907
just about everything that he did.

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:03.943
He realized that part of it

00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:05.411
is about the cards that you're dealt,

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:06.012
and part of it's

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:07.247
about the things that you know.

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:08.881
But the bigger part of the game

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:10.483
really is about the relationships that you have

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:11.150
with other people:

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.019
your ability to be able to read what somebody else

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:14.520
is going to be able to give you

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.823
and to take a compromise when you can,

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:17.757
and some of the times know

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.093
that it's time to walk away from the game.

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:22.562
Ed Zigler had an amazing ability to work

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.063
across sectors.

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:25.598
He and several of his colleagues

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:27.133
from various disciplines

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.235
established the field of child development

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.070
and social policy.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.405
He and I co-authored

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:34.841
the first two graduate textbooks that

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:35.875
defined the field.

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:38.244
I'm extremely

00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:39.746
thankful on so many levels

00:04:39.746 --> 00:04:41.981
for Ed Zigler's impact.

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:43.750
Of course, he's one of the most

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:46.953
impactful psychologists of the last 100 years,

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:51.157
really making an extraordinary transition

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:52.992
from mastering and contributing

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:54.961
to basic developmental science,

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:56.329
and then moving to applied

00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:57.930
developmental science and essentially

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.099
creating the field of child development

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.302
and public policy.

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:04.437
The field of intellectual disability

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:07.307
owes its origins to Dr. Ed Zigler.

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:10.710
And the work of the Special Olympics Movement:

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.979
You know, my mom, the founder

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:14.247
of the Special Olympics Movement, 

00:05:14.247 --> 00:05:17.016
was always indebted to Ed, always,

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:17.984
throughout her life,

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:19.585
and called on him frequently 

00:05:19.585 --> 00:05:21.888
to ask for advice on the design

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:24.557
of programs, the evaluation of programs,

00:05:24.557 --> 00:05:25.458
how to promote

00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:26.993
and explain to policymakers

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:29.329
the value of these kinds of interventions.

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:33.066
Before Head Start, there were programs

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:35.868
for poor children in this country.

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:37.737
I think our Susan Gray’s

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:39.972
project down in Nashville, 

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:43.676
Deutsch's project in New York City.

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:47.280
There was actually one in New Haven

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:50.917
that was sponsored by the Ford Foundation.

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:55.288
But what characterized those programs

00:05:56.656 --> 00:05:58.424
was they were interested

00:05:58.424 --> 00:06:00.693
only in education,

00:06:01.928 --> 00:06:05.231
only in education.

00:06:05.498 --> 00:06:06.466
And

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:09.769
what Head Start did was it introduced, really,

00:06:09.769 --> 00:06:11.604
the “whole child” approach.

00:06:11.604 --> 00:06:12.538
The “whole child” concept

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.375
first was articulated back in 1930

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:17.377
in a conference on children

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:19.379
that was being held by the White House.

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:20.780
And it was a concept

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:22.715
that was kicked around and people talked about

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:23.883
and I think people understood

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:24.717
the importance of it.

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:25.852
It's ironic

00:06:25.852 --> 00:06:26.486
that it was first

00:06:26.486 --> 00:06:27.687
mentioned in 1930,

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:29.922
the very year that Ed Zigler was born.

00:06:30.256 --> 00:06:32.058
But it wasn't really until Head Start

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:33.393
that anybody actually took it

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:34.861
serious enough to actually do

00:06:34.861 --> 00:06:36.662
anything with that concept.

00:06:36.662 --> 00:06:38.030
It was an academic concept

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.133
that Ed turned into a reality in terms

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:41.701
of how we treat children and families.

00:06:42.802 --> 00:06:43.636
Sadly, there are

00:06:43.636 --> 00:06:44.637
little children

00:06:44.637 --> 00:06:45.505
who are already headed

00:06:45.505 --> 00:06:47.840
for lives of frustration and misery.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.675
No one ever read them a story;

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:51.310
taught them a nursery rhyme;

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:54.480
showed them about colors, letters and numbers.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.151
Beginning in 1964, Sarge Shriver,

00:06:58.151 --> 00:07:01.187
who was the head of the War on Poverty, really

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:05.691
had this big committee that planned Head Start.

00:07:06.726 --> 00:07:08.361
I was the youngest member.

00:07:11.464 --> 00:07:13.766
“One foot right in front of the other.” 

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:16.936
The thing that I remember the most

00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:18.671
was about the wisdom

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:20.973
that went into the planning of this program.

00:07:21.507 --> 00:07:22.608
And it all started

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:24.043
with the convening of a group

00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:26.012
called the Cooke Committee,

00:07:26.012 --> 00:07:28.448
and they had the wisdom to see that

00:07:28.448 --> 00:07:30.183
you needed a healthy child

00:07:30.183 --> 00:07:30.917
to have a child

00:07:30.917 --> 00:07:32.852
that would be prepared to learn,

00:07:32.852 --> 00:07:34.954
that you had to involve the parents to really

00:07:36.022 --> 00:07:37.523
be supportive of that child's

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:38.791
development and learning,

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:42.061
and also that the parents, in turn, would learn

00:07:42.328 --> 00:07:45.631
from being part of such a comprehensive program.

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:47.934
The Planning Committee was made up

00:07:48.067 --> 00:07:51.471
of leading scholars from diverse disciplines,

00:07:52.338 --> 00:07:54.540
including preschool education,

00:07:55.508 --> 00:07:57.677
pediatrics,

00:07:57.677 --> 00:07:59.946
child development,

00:07:59.946 --> 00:08:02.582
clinical psychology,

00:08:02.582 --> 00:08:05.218
social work and nursing.

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:08.054
The Planning Committee's

00:08:08.054 --> 00:08:10.723
 overarching goal for Head Start

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.559
was the optimal development

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:14.760
of the children

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:17.029
experiencing the Head Start program.

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.433
The Planning Committee, unfortunately,

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:21.434
provided

00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:23.703
a very long list of subgoals

00:08:23.703 --> 00:08:27.306
to reach this major goal, and this long list

00:08:27.773 --> 00:08:29.108
resulted in the clear

00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:32.445
goal of Head Start remaining ambiguous.

00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:36.182
I myself, then in my own scholarship,

00:08:36.616 --> 00:08:38.284
decided to simplify

00:08:38.284 --> 00:08:41.621
by designating the four systems

00:08:41.821 --> 00:08:45.157
that were absolutely most critical

00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:47.727
in impacting human development.

00:08:48.661 --> 00:08:52.365
First and foremost is the family system.

00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:55.568
The other three

00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:58.504
being the health system,

00:08:58.771 --> 00:09:01.741
the education system,

00:09:01.741 --> 00:09:04.443
and the child care system,

00:09:04.443 --> 00:09:06.445
where so many children

00:09:06.445 --> 00:09:09.081
spend those critical early years of life.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:12.285
We had this big ballroom

00:09:12.285 --> 00:09:14.186
in the New Colonial Hotel,

00:09:14.186 --> 00:09:16.255
 this old, battered hotel.

00:09:16.689 --> 00:09:19.025
We didn't even have file cabinets.

00:09:19.025 --> 00:09:21.160
Applications began pouring in.

00:09:22.395 --> 00:09:24.330
We used to keep the applications

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:26.699
in— Off this ballroom were restrooms,

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:30.870
and in them were bathtubs, and we kept

00:09:31.971 --> 00:09:35.341
the applications in those bathtubs.

00:09:35.341 --> 00:09:37.209
Now, what Jule Sugarman did

00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:40.880
that was really brilliant was hire substitute

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.249
schoolteachers, and they would sit there,

00:09:44.317 --> 00:09:47.019
and they would go through the applications.

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:49.522
And if they had an educational component,

00:09:49.522 --> 00:09:50.256
a health component,

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:52.391
if they had all the components of Head Start,

00:09:52.892 --> 00:09:53.793
they would check it

00:09:53.793 --> 00:09:55.127
and there would be like five checks.

00:09:55.127 --> 00:09:57.797
And if they give could them the five checks, 

00:09:58.531 --> 00:10:00.533
the check for Head Start would go out to them.

00:10:00.533 --> 00:10:01.767
So, in

00:10:01.767 --> 00:10:04.704
doing that, you know that you had to put in

00:10:04.737 --> 00:10:08.040
place a lot of poor programs.

00:10:09.108 --> 00:10:10.443
Ed realized very early on

00:10:10.443 --> 00:10:11.911
that there needed to be a way to be able

00:10:11.911 --> 00:10:12.645
to bring people

00:10:12.645 --> 00:10:14.480
into the profession of caring

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:16.248
for children and families.

00:10:16.248 --> 00:10:17.516
And that's where he came up

00:10:17.516 --> 00:10:19.285
with the idea of the CDA,

00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:21.287
the Child Development Associate Credential.

00:10:22.221 --> 00:10:24.557
We are still having the conversation

00:10:24.557 --> 00:10:25.658
about what it means to create

00:10:25.658 --> 00:10:26.425
a professional field

00:10:26.425 --> 00:10:27.927
of practice for early childhood

00:10:27.927 --> 00:10:29.595
educators, the competencies

00:10:29.595 --> 00:10:31.597
and standards of early childhood education.

00:10:31.964 --> 00:10:34.233
And I can't tell you how many NAEYC members

00:10:34.233 --> 00:10:36.369
and how many individuals

00:10:36.369 --> 00:10:39.305
who I’ve come in contact with who will talk

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:41.307
about the very first thing

00:10:41.307 --> 00:10:41.941
that they did

00:10:41.941 --> 00:10:43.376
was to get their CDA

00:10:43.376 --> 00:10:45.711
and what that did in helping them understand

00:10:45.711 --> 00:10:47.980
how deeply they cared about this profession,

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:50.049
how they wanted to make a career out of it,

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:52.818
and how it became the jumping off point

00:10:52.818 --> 00:10:56.255
for these extraordinary women and men

00:10:56.522 --> 00:10:58.557
who are now leaders, 

00:10:58.557 --> 00:11:02.194
system leaders, educator leaders, in our field.

00:11:02.194 --> 00:11:03.529
It's really quite remarkable.

00:11:03.529 --> 00:11:06.766
So, Dr. Zigler's legacy lives on.

00:11:06.766 --> 00:11:07.500
It's an honor

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:11.037
and a privilege to celebrate his life.

00:11:11.537 --> 00:11:14.073
We originally thought we would enroll

00:11:14.073 --> 00:11:17.810
25,000 children in that first summer program.

00:11:18.544 --> 00:11:21.113
Applications for Head Start

00:11:21.681 --> 00:11:24.116
began pouring into OEO,

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:27.486
and we wound up enrolling over

00:11:27.486 --> 00:11:30.723
560,000 children

00:11:31.457 --> 00:11:33.359
in that first summer.

00:11:35.161 --> 00:11:35.795
Head Start

00:11:35.795 --> 00:11:38.531
was also very popular at the White House.

00:11:39.331 --> 00:11:42.902
Lady Bird Johnson was the honorary chairperson

00:11:42.902 --> 00:11:46.172
of Head Start, and President Johnson,

00:11:47.173 --> 00:11:49.742
who was himself an ex-teacher,

00:11:49.742 --> 00:11:52.144
was very enamored with Head Start.

00:11:53.112 --> 00:11:56.082
The person who originally ran Head Start

00:11:56.582 --> 00:11:58.417
was Julie Richmond.

00:11:58.417 --> 00:12:00.286
Julie was a pediatrician

00:12:00.286 --> 00:12:06.926
and my mentor, a brilliant, brilliant man.

00:12:06.926 --> 00:12:08.527
At the age of 35,

00:12:08.527 --> 00:12:10.696
I was hired by President Nixon

00:12:11.697 --> 00:12:14.834
to run Head Start and to run the Children's Bureau.

00:12:15.501 --> 00:12:18.070
I was Nixon's agent for all 

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:22.541
children’s programs in the United States,

00:12:22.541 --> 00:12:24.243
which the most important one

00:12:24.243 --> 00:12:26.946
and the largest being Head Start.

00:12:26.946 --> 00:12:29.582
I wrote about Ed in my book Preschool Education

00:12:29.582 --> 00:12:30.816
in America,

00:12:30.816 --> 00:12:32.351
reviewed his books,

00:12:32.351 --> 00:12:34.920
and interviewed him for publications,

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:35.888
including a piece

00:12:35.888 --> 00:12:37.490
we wrote together

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:40.059
on his early days at Head Start.

00:12:40.059 --> 00:12:42.762
As he talked about when President Richard M.

00:12:42.762 --> 00:12:43.562
Nixon

00:12:44.196 --> 00:12:46.365
vetoed the Comprehensive Child

00:12:46.365 --> 00:12:49.935
Development Act in 1971, Ed's

00:12:50.169 --> 00:12:53.572
deep knowledge of policy research

00:12:54.173 --> 00:12:56.208
all the players came out,

00:12:57.009 --> 00:12:59.411
but he also knew the inside story.

00:12:59.411 --> 00:13:01.480
He knew the bureaucratic infighting,

00:13:01.847 --> 00:13:05.151
and he talked about it in a way that was candid

00:13:05.351 --> 00:13:06.018
and witty.

00:13:07.186 --> 00:13:08.020
Ed would give you

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:11.791
his opinions, rationales, suggestions,

00:13:12.124 --> 00:13:15.060
and a wealth of evidence to back up his views

00:13:15.694 --> 00:13:17.963
in an open way.

00:13:17.963 --> 00:13:19.765
As he said,

00:13:19.865 --> 00:13:22.368
his politics were children.

00:13:23.435 --> 00:13:26.939
His goal was always to extend child welfare.

00:13:27.873 --> 00:13:30.075
Upon taking charge of Head Start,

00:13:31.844 --> 00:13:34.313
I immediately announced

00:13:34.313 --> 00:13:36.415
that Head Start would mount

00:13:37.249 --> 00:13:40.786
an improvement and innovation effort,

00:13:41.954 --> 00:13:44.023
and that Head Start would become

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:47.760
a national laboratory

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:49.728
in which we would mount and assess

00:13:49.728 --> 00:13:51.964
programs holding promise

00:13:53.098 --> 00:13:56.101
for improving the development of children

00:13:56.802 --> 00:13:59.338
living in poverty.

00:13:59.605 --> 00:14:01.140
In my book, "Head

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:02.107
Start and Beyond",

00:14:02.107 --> 00:14:06.078
which I wrote some 20 years ago, I spelled out

00:14:06.912 --> 00:14:09.181
what a model program

00:14:09.181 --> 00:14:12.051
for poor children would be:

00:14:12.051 --> 00:14:15.721
a three-year conception to age three program

00:14:15.721 --> 00:14:18.791
for infants and toddlers, like Early Head Start.

00:14:20.226 --> 00:14:23.562
Then you follow this with a high-quality

00:14:23.562 --> 00:14:27.800
two-year, full-day, full-year program,

00:14:29.568 --> 00:14:32.004
which provides poor children

00:14:32.004 --> 00:14:34.273
the preschool education they need

00:14:35.241 --> 00:14:37.977
while providing Head Start parents

00:14:38.644 --> 00:14:40.880
high-quality child care

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:43.115
they need so they can work.

00:14:43.883 --> 00:14:45.451
In 1972,

00:14:45.451 --> 00:14:46.785
I was a research associate

00:14:46.785 --> 00:14:48.988
at HighScope in Ypsilanti, Michigan,

00:14:48.988 --> 00:14:49.722
beginning work

00:14:49.722 --> 00:14:52.391
on my first early childhood program evaluation.

00:14:53.025 --> 00:14:54.560
It was a study of the new Home

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.695
Start Demonstration Project.

00:14:56.695 --> 00:14:58.430
I was not aware of at the time,

00:14:58.430 --> 00:14:59.632
but that was to be important

00:14:59.632 --> 00:15:01.867
through the years in my relationship with Ed.

00:15:03.035 --> 00:15:04.336
In the early seventies,

00:15:04.336 --> 00:15:05.671
he and others in what was then

00:15:05.671 --> 00:15:07.406
the Office of Child Development

00:15:07.406 --> 00:15:09.708
in the old Department of Health, Education

00:15:09.708 --> 00:15:10.943
and Welfare

00:15:10.943 --> 00:15:12.478
were developing the first Head

00:15:12.478 --> 00:15:14.513
Start Policy Manual.

00:15:14.513 --> 00:15:16.282
Head Start’s most serious

00:15:16.282 --> 00:15:19.351
problem has always been inconsistent quality.

00:15:20.319 --> 00:15:23.255
Some programs are excellent, some are mediocre,

00:15:23.589 --> 00:15:24.657
and a few are poor.

00:15:26.425 --> 00:15:28.494
This situation began

00:15:28.494 --> 00:15:30.863
before the first center opened its doors.

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:35.200
The project started off so big and so fast

00:15:35.901 --> 00:15:38.404
that quality controls were left behind.

00:15:39.104 --> 00:15:42.408
From 1965 to 1970,

00:15:42.875 --> 00:15:45.644
Head Start had absolutely no

00:15:46.111 --> 00:15:49.048
performance standards.

00:15:49.048 --> 00:15:51.917
Every Head Start center was essentially doing

00:15:52.318 --> 00:15:54.920
whatever it cared to do and whatever they felt

00:15:55.154 --> 00:15:58.290
was Head Start.

00:15:58.290 --> 00:15:59.024
I thus,

00:15:59.024 --> 00:16:01.727
with the very first performance standards,

00:16:01.727 --> 00:16:02.361
which lasted

00:16:02.361 --> 00:16:03.996
till the Clinton Administration

00:16:03.996 --> 00:16:05.397
when they were updated,

00:16:05.397 --> 00:16:06.699
guaranteed that Head

00:16:06.699 --> 00:16:08.600
Start was a genuine program

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:10.602
in which every Head Start center

00:16:10.602 --> 00:16:13.472
did a certain set of activities.

00:16:14.173 --> 00:16:15.841
And I and my staff

00:16:15.841 --> 00:16:18.577
created the Head Start Policy Councils,

00:16:19.445 --> 00:16:23.048
which must consist at least half of parents.

00:16:24.049 --> 00:16:27.019
And these Policy Councils still exist

00:16:27.386 --> 00:16:28.854
and are the ruling body

00:16:28.854 --> 00:16:30.689
of each Head Start center.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:33.325
Giving parents this power

00:16:33.325 --> 00:16:34.493
has made parents

00:16:34.493 --> 00:16:37.563
the most effective advocates for Head Start.

00:16:38.297 --> 00:16:40.366
When President Carter

00:16:40.366 --> 00:16:43.202
tried to move Head Start from Health, Education

00:16:43.202 --> 00:16:47.339
and Welfare to the new Department of Education,

00:16:47.339 --> 00:16:49.041
this would have destroyed Head Start

00:16:49.041 --> 00:16:50.609
as we know it.

00:16:50.743 --> 00:16:53.812
And it was parents who interceded

00:16:55.214 --> 00:16:58.851
and kept Head Start in HEW

00:16:59.251 --> 00:17:02.721
where it has had a comfortable life.

00:17:02.721 --> 00:17:04.289
Poker is assessing people

00:17:04.289 --> 00:17:05.190
when they're bluffing,

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:06.191
when they're not bluffing.

00:17:06.191 --> 00:17:09.428
That happens in Washington all the time.

00:17:09.428 --> 00:17:12.598
They might try to throw a bluff up at you.

00:17:13.465 --> 00:17:15.701
Like that guy at OMB did.

00:17:15.701 --> 00:17:17.536
That was just a bluff.

00:17:17.536 --> 00:17:19.772
One of the most significant initiatives

00:17:19.772 --> 00:17:21.040
that Ed and I worked on

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:23.175
is the School of the 21st Century

00:17:23.175 --> 00:17:25.811
which forever changed the way schools operate.

00:17:26.445 --> 00:17:28.313
Schools begin to serve children,

00:17:28.313 --> 00:17:29.915
the birth of the child

00:17:29.915 --> 00:17:33.452
through middle school and adolescence.

00:17:33.452 --> 00:17:34.686
They also provide

00:17:34.686 --> 00:17:36.055
various support services

00:17:36.055 --> 00:17:37.456
in addition to academics.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:40.626
Millions of schools around the country

00:17:40.793 --> 00:17:42.828
are Schools of the 21st Century,

00:17:42.828 --> 00:17:44.696
and the program also spawns

00:17:44.696 --> 00:17:46.832
similar initiatives.

00:17:46.832 --> 00:17:48.534
Dr. Zigler conceptualized

00:17:48.534 --> 00:17:50.803
what is now known as Schools of the 21st

00:17:50.803 --> 00:17:52.404
Century, or 21C.

00:17:52.404 --> 00:17:55.441
Now in over 1300 communities nationwide

00:17:55.441 --> 00:17:57.709
in rural, urban and suburban areas,

00:17:57.910 --> 00:17:59.244
with year-round services

00:17:59.244 --> 00:18:01.747
beginning at birth and focusing on the evolving

00:18:01.747 --> 00:18:02.981
needs of the whole child

00:18:02.981 --> 00:18:05.484
through age 12, 21C schools

00:18:05.484 --> 00:18:07.186
create an invaluable platform

00:18:07.186 --> 00:18:09.888
to build on for success in school and beyond.

00:18:10.456 --> 00:18:13.425
The sad fact is somewhere on 35%

00:18:13.425 --> 00:18:15.994
of all of our children arrive at school

00:18:17.096 --> 00:18:18.831
not optimally ready to profit

00:18:18.831 --> 00:18:20.399
from the school experience.

00:18:20.399 --> 00:18:21.700
Too many children are simply

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:24.169
not getting a good preschool education.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:26.338
Poor quality of child care

00:18:26.338 --> 00:18:28.240
is compromising their growth and development.

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:30.375
So that if we can just use the school

00:18:30.375 --> 00:18:31.076
as a site

00:18:31.076 --> 00:18:33.545
where children can get good preschool

00:18:33.712 --> 00:18:36.448
education and child care as well,

00:18:36.782 --> 00:18:37.783
that would be a void

00:18:37.783 --> 00:18:39.852
that is badly in need of filling.

00:18:39.852 --> 00:18:41.386
The idea of the School of the 21st

00:18:41.386 --> 00:18:43.021
Century is “why

00:18:43.021 --> 00:18:44.123
should children go to school,

00:18:44.123 --> 00:18:46.091
and then have to go to the doctor's office

00:18:46.091 --> 00:18:46.758
someplace else

00:18:46.758 --> 00:18:48.193
or the dentist's office someplace else?”

00:18:48.193 --> 00:18:49.428
We know where the children are

00:18:49.428 --> 00:18:50.629
—they’re in the schools.

00:18:50.629 --> 00:18:51.864
That's where we should be putting

00:18:51.864 --> 00:18:52.998
all of the services

00:18:52.998 --> 00:18:54.299
that are affecting the lives of children

00:18:54.299 --> 00:18:55.934
and families, right there where they are,

00:18:55.934 --> 00:18:56.969
in the schools.

00:18:56.969 --> 00:19:01.173
The Comer-Zigler Initiative is really an effort

00:19:01.406 --> 00:19:05.544
to support the development of children in a way

00:19:05.878 --> 00:19:09.181
that they want to learn and be successful

00:19:09.481 --> 00:19:12.651
and they want to manage themselves in a way

00:19:13.118 --> 00:19:15.420
that is respectful, helpful, useful

00:19:15.721 --> 00:19:18.023
to themselves and to others.

00:19:18.023 --> 00:19:20.025
The way our program came about

00:19:20.025 --> 00:19:22.561
is that we were working in the same building

00:19:22.561 --> 00:19:23.529
but separately

00:19:23.529 --> 00:19:24.630
and had never thought

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:26.632
about trying to work together.

00:19:26.632 --> 00:19:29.101
But we had the same foundation executive

00:19:29.101 --> 00:19:31.036
working with us, and

00:19:32.104 --> 00:19:33.772
it occurred to him that if

00:19:33.772 --> 00:19:35.541
we put the two programs together,

00:19:35.541 --> 00:19:37.676
we would be even stronger.

00:19:37.676 --> 00:19:39.778
That program was so successful

00:19:39.778 --> 00:19:41.113
that they were accused

00:19:41.113 --> 00:19:43.348
of cheating on the state tests,

00:19:43.348 --> 00:19:45.250
and they had to take it over again,

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:47.953
and they did better the second time

00:19:48.253 --> 00:19:49.922
than they did the first.

00:19:49.922 --> 00:19:52.758
It wasn't cheating.

00:19:52.758 --> 00:19:54.092
It was child development.

00:19:54.092 --> 00:19:55.827
It was support for child development.

00:19:55.827 --> 00:19:57.863
It was reorganizing the system

00:19:58.263 --> 00:20:00.132
so that you could

00:20:00.299 --> 00:20:03.202
create an experience for the children

00:20:03.602 --> 00:20:07.105
that everybody developed, and they could learn.

00:20:07.606 --> 00:20:10.209
Ed was very much in favor

00:20:10.209 --> 00:20:13.045
of having graduated rates.

00:20:13.445 --> 00:20:15.280
Namely, that he would allow there

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.249
to be universal pre-K,

00:20:17.249 --> 00:20:18.150
but he knew that

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:19.651
there would be a waste of money

00:20:19.651 --> 00:20:21.587
if we gave this pre-K

00:20:21.587 --> 00:20:23.488
to very affluent families.

00:20:23.488 --> 00:20:24.723
First of all, a lot of affluent

00:20:24.723 --> 00:20:26.258
families wouldn't take it.

00:20:26.258 --> 00:20:28.860
And secondly, that they could pay in a way

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:30.829
that disadvantaged families couldn't.

00:20:30.829 --> 00:20:31.597
So, he was very,

00:20:31.597 --> 00:20:33.899
very enthusiastic about the idea

00:20:34.299 --> 00:20:35.968
of having a graduated scale.

00:20:35.968 --> 00:20:38.503
And to an economist, this is like a payment

00:20:39.037 --> 00:20:40.439
schedule based on needs.

00:20:40.439 --> 00:20:42.074
And so I thought

00:20:42.074 --> 00:20:44.476
he made some very useful contributions to me,

00:20:44.476 --> 00:20:47.279
and I incorporated that in some of my own work.

00:20:47.279 --> 00:20:49.514
He made a real difference

00:20:49.514 --> 00:20:53.552
in terms of social policy in the United States.

00:20:54.486 --> 00:20:56.088
He was smart.

00:20:56.088 --> 00:20:57.422
He was savvy.

00:20:57.422 --> 00:20:59.091
He was connected.

00:20:59.091 --> 00:21:01.627
He knew how to bridge academia

00:21:01.994 --> 00:21:03.295
in the real world.

00:21:03.295 --> 00:21:05.330
He was one of a handful of people

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:06.531
that had it all

00:21:06.531 --> 00:21:08.734
when it came to early childhood.

00:21:08.734 --> 00:21:10.936
He understood the policies.

00:21:10.969 --> 00:21:13.739
He understood the mechanics of early childhood

00:21:13.739 --> 00:21:16.742
and why it was failing, in many ways,

00:21:17.442 --> 00:21:18.710
many of our families.

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:20.512
And in addition to that,

00:21:20.512 --> 00:21:22.948
he understood the politics of Washington.

00:21:23.348 --> 00:21:27.753
In 1978 Ed had been back from Washington, D.C.

00:21:27.753 --> 00:21:29.187
for about six years

00:21:29.187 --> 00:21:31.290
and had been completely convinced

00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:32.457
that the biggest challenge

00:21:32.457 --> 00:21:35.360
that we have in applied child development

00:21:35.661 --> 00:21:36.862
is that people who understand

00:21:36.862 --> 00:21:38.330
child development don't understand

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:40.399
much about policy, and people who understand

00:21:40.399 --> 00:21:41.800
and are making policy decisions

00:21:41.800 --> 00:21:42.501
on behalf of children

00:21:42.501 --> 00:21:44.403
and families don't know much about the science

00:21:44.403 --> 00:21:45.737
of child development.

00:21:45.737 --> 00:21:48.340
And so in 1978, he got a grant

00:21:48.573 --> 00:21:51.410
from the Archibald Bush Foundation of Minnesota

00:21:51.677 --> 00:21:53.312
to create the Bush Center

00:21:53.312 --> 00:21:55.347
in Child Development and Social Policy.

00:21:55.347 --> 00:21:56.815
At the Bush Foundation,

00:21:56.815 --> 00:21:58.750
we invest in great ideas

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:01.086
and the people who power them.

00:22:01.086 --> 00:22:02.788
That is our tagline.

00:22:02.788 --> 00:22:04.690
And it's also our strategy.

00:22:05.657 --> 00:22:07.626
And marrying

00:22:07.626 --> 00:22:10.329
child development research

00:22:10.329 --> 00:22:12.631
with policy formation,

00:22:12.631 --> 00:22:15.901
translating the science into strategies

00:22:15.901 --> 00:22:19.171
to support the well-being of kids

00:22:19.171 --> 00:22:22.441
and families, is a great idea.

00:22:23.508 --> 00:22:25.711
The investments we made

00:22:25.944 --> 00:22:28.513
in the creation of the Yale Center

00:22:29.014 --> 00:22:32.384
from way back in 1977 are significant

00:22:32.384 --> 00:22:34.820
in the history of the Bush Foundation.

00:22:35.354 --> 00:22:39.391
And in our future, we're excited to continue

00:22:39.391 --> 00:22:42.594
to see the long-term impact of this work.

00:22:44.062 --> 00:22:45.597
The Smith Richardson Foundation's

00:22:45.597 --> 00:22:49.000
long relationship with Ed Zigler began in 1986

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:50.102
when he helped the foundation

00:22:50.102 --> 00:22:51.169
launch its Children

00:22:51.169 --> 00:22:53.405
and Families at Risk Program.

00:22:53.405 --> 00:22:54.172
At the time,

00:22:54.172 --> 00:22:55.474
the foundation was concerned

00:22:55.474 --> 00:22:56.274
about the well-being

00:22:56.274 --> 00:22:58.443
of children living in unstable families

00:22:58.443 --> 00:22:59.644
with low incomes

00:22:59.644 --> 00:23:01.880
and limited opportunities for upward mobility.

00:23:02.547 --> 00:23:05.083
We asked several leading social policy experts

00:23:05.083 --> 00:23:06.518
to identify scholars

00:23:06.518 --> 00:23:07.152
who understood

00:23:07.152 --> 00:23:09.221
best what kinds of public policies

00:23:09.221 --> 00:23:10.922
and programmatic interventions

00:23:10.922 --> 00:23:12.090
could most effectively promote

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:13.592
the healthy development of children

00:23:13.592 --> 00:23:15.394
from disadvantaged backgrounds.

00:23:15.394 --> 00:23:16.762
They all pointed to Ed.

00:23:16.762 --> 00:23:18.029
It was really Ed Zigler's

00:23:18.029 --> 00:23:19.865
vision that created 

00:23:19.865 --> 00:23:21.466
the Bush Center in Child

00:23:21.466 --> 00:23:23.168
Development and Social Policy at Yale

00:23:23.168 --> 00:23:24.236
that we renamed

00:23:24.236 --> 00:23:25.270
the Edward Zigler Center

00:23:25.270 --> 00:23:28.173
in Child Development and Social Policy in 2005.

00:23:28.273 --> 00:23:30.542
It was a way to be able to remember his legacy,

00:23:30.542 --> 00:23:34.246
but it was also a way to be able to remind us

00:23:34.246 --> 00:23:37.149
all of the fact that a good vision,

00:23:37.516 --> 00:23:40.652
a good imagination can create things

00:23:40.652 --> 00:23:42.154
that can have a huge impact

00:23:42.154 --> 00:23:44.256
on everyone else that follows,

00:23:44.256 --> 00:23:46.324
simply because you're impacting the way

00:23:46.324 --> 00:23:48.326
in which they think about things.

00:23:48.326 --> 00:23:51.396
The Zigler Center has changed the trajectory

00:23:51.396 --> 00:23:53.432
of many people at Yale

00:23:53.432 --> 00:23:55.801
who have participated in it—taken people

00:23:55.801 --> 00:23:56.802
who understood

00:23:56.802 --> 00:23:58.136
and were interested in science

00:23:58.136 --> 00:24:00.005
and made them very fascinated

00:24:00.005 --> 00:24:01.606
about how to move policy;

00:24:01.606 --> 00:24:03.308
people who understood policy

00:24:03.308 --> 00:24:05.410
but didn't know much about child development

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:07.012
and helped them be able to gain the skills

00:24:07.012 --> 00:24:07.612
that they needed

00:24:07.612 --> 00:24:09.181
in order to be able to be

00:24:09.181 --> 00:24:10.115
the kind of people

00:24:10.115 --> 00:24:12.484
who would be the leaders of the future.

00:24:12.484 --> 00:24:16.888
I think in all of the people that I have met,

00:24:17.055 --> 00:24:22.027
Ed combined these incredible ways of being,

00:24:22.794 --> 00:24:24.963
He eminently was grounded

00:24:24.963 --> 00:24:27.065
in his very humble beginnings,

00:24:27.499 --> 00:24:29.234
and he was sort of scrappy

00:24:29.234 --> 00:24:31.069
till the very end

00:24:31.069 --> 00:24:33.271
because he always had to fight for everything.

00:24:33.705 --> 00:24:35.173
And yet at the same time,

00:24:35.173 --> 00:24:36.374
his being was hallmarked

00:24:36.374 --> 00:24:38.610
by this consummate intelligence.

00:24:39.044 --> 00:24:40.545
You would tell him something,

00:24:40.545 --> 00:24:41.847
and he would remember it

00:24:41.847 --> 00:24:46.051
a half a decade later, or he would come up

00:24:46.051 --> 00:24:49.488
with profound ways of thinking afresh

00:24:49.488 --> 00:24:50.789
about something.

00:24:50.789 --> 00:24:52.591
So that I would say that

00:24:52.591 --> 00:24:54.726
of all the people that I've known (and

00:24:54.726 --> 00:24:55.694
probably most people

00:24:55.694 --> 00:24:57.229
who've come in contact with him),

00:24:57.229 --> 00:25:00.799
it was that combination of drive

00:25:00.799 --> 00:25:04.069
and energy and inventiveness and intelligence

00:25:04.569 --> 00:25:08.440
that really made him so incredibly special,

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:11.543
so incredibly valuable to Yale and to

00:25:11.543 --> 00:25:13.545
the entire community that cares about children

00:25:13.545 --> 00:25:16.047
and families in this country and in others.

00:25:16.982 --> 00:25:19.184
Ed knew from his research that

00:25:20.185 --> 00:25:21.453
the baby's first

00:25:21.453 --> 00:25:22.921
teacher is their parent

00:25:22.921 --> 00:25:23.455
and that they

00:25:23.455 --> 00:25:25.257
need to form a strong relationship.

00:25:25.257 --> 00:25:27.092
Mothers and fathers need to form

00:25:27.092 --> 00:25:28.026
a strong relationship

00:25:28.026 --> 00:25:29.160
with their babies, and their babies

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:30.362
so need to be able to form

00:25:30.362 --> 00:25:31.429
that relationship, too.

00:25:31.429 --> 00:25:34.466
And that's why the very first days of a baby's

00:25:34.466 --> 00:25:37.235
life are so deeply, deeply important.

00:25:37.536 --> 00:25:40.605
And that's why he cared about parental leave

00:25:40.605 --> 00:25:42.541
and parents being able to take the time off from work

00:25:42.541 --> 00:25:44.609
to be able to form that strong bond

00:25:44.609 --> 00:25:46.111
with their baby.

00:25:46.111 --> 00:25:47.846
He also realized in 1993

00:25:47.846 --> 00:25:48.513
he wasn't going to be able

00:25:48.513 --> 00:25:49.848
to get a paid parental leave.

00:25:49.848 --> 00:25:51.216
So, he took a compromise

00:25:51.216 --> 00:25:52.984
and he worked with Chris Dodd and other people

00:25:52.984 --> 00:25:54.019
in Washington, D.C.

00:25:54.019 --> 00:25:57.022
to create the Family Medical Leave Act in 1993,

00:25:57.289 --> 00:25:58.557
which wasn't paid

00:25:58.557 --> 00:26:00.959
but nonetheless protected a parent’s ability

00:26:00.959 --> 00:26:02.360
to be able to take time off

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:05.030
and form that important bond with their child.

00:26:05.363 --> 00:26:06.765
It took me seven years to pass

00:26:06.765 --> 00:26:09.100
family medical leave without pay.

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:11.136
It was vetoed two or three times.

00:26:11.136 --> 00:26:12.904
And Ed, of course, was terrific help on that

00:26:12.904 --> 00:26:14.139
and could explain it in ways

00:26:14.139 --> 00:26:15.307
that people would understand.

00:26:15.307 --> 00:26:17.108
I was talking to Bill Clinton

00:26:17.108 --> 00:26:18.043
several years ago

00:26:18.043 --> 00:26:20.178
on the anniversary of the signing of the bill.

00:26:20.178 --> 00:26:21.680
It was the first piece of legislation

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.648
he signed into law as President.

00:26:23.648 --> 00:26:25.584
He said, as a former president,

00:26:25.584 --> 00:26:27.485
people stop me all the time and say hello

00:26:27.485 --> 00:26:28.320
and this, that and the other.

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:29.521
He said, the one issue

00:26:29.521 --> 00:26:31.856
they bring up more than any other issue

00:26:31.856 --> 00:26:34.025
was the fact that I was the President

00:26:34.025 --> 00:26:35.460
that signed into law

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:36.795
the Family Medical Leave Act.

00:26:36.795 --> 00:26:38.229
Of all the things I was involved

00:26:38.229 --> 00:26:39.331
in, that's the one thing

00:26:39.331 --> 00:26:40.899
they bring up more than anything else.

00:26:40.899 --> 00:26:43.768
What we're realizing now is that it's critical

00:26:43.768 --> 00:26:45.770
that families have access to a paid

00:26:45.770 --> 00:26:48.073
leave in order to make it possible

00:26:48.073 --> 00:26:49.741
for them to actually take advantage

00:26:49.741 --> 00:26:52.410
of the provisions under the FMLA.

00:26:53.211 --> 00:26:55.113
And that research

00:26:55.113 --> 00:26:58.183
from the 1970s that he and his students did

00:26:58.183 --> 00:27:01.052
is still the research that people are citing.

00:27:01.686 --> 00:27:03.121
And today

00:27:03.121 --> 00:27:03.855
we have over

00:27:03.855 --> 00:27:04.589
eight states

00:27:04.589 --> 00:27:08.360
that now have a fully paid parental leave,

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:09.661
and the federal government

00:27:09.661 --> 00:27:11.529
has it for service workers.

00:27:11.529 --> 00:27:12.731
We're knocking at the door

00:27:12.731 --> 00:27:16.401
now on a vision that Ed had back in 1993.

00:27:16.801 --> 00:27:19.838
He supported us in founding the Yale

00:27:19.838 --> 00:27:22.974
China Program on Child Development

00:27:22.974 --> 00:27:24.676
at Yale University.

00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:30.482
Dr. Zigler had a lifelong commitment to providing

00:27:30.715 --> 00:27:33.718
the “whole child” approach to education.

00:27:34.619 --> 00:27:37.022
As in Head Start, the Yale

00:27:37.022 --> 00:27:39.591
China Program on Child Development

00:27:40.058 --> 00:27:42.894
aims to provide children a good start,

00:27:43.261 --> 00:27:46.798
through cross-cultural academic collaborations.

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:56.675
Above everything else,

00:27:56.675 --> 00:27:58.043
Ed cared most deeply

00:27:58.043 --> 00:28:00.311
about teaching and mentoring

00:28:00.311 --> 00:28:02.180
and making the next generation

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:04.215
of leaders to be able to carry on the work

00:28:04.215 --> 00:28:06.351
and carry the work forward.

00:28:06.351 --> 00:28:08.553
I had the honor of being in Ed's orbit

00:28:08.553 --> 00:28:09.988
for 21 years,

00:28:09.988 --> 00:28:11.456
first as a graduate student

00:28:11.456 --> 00:28:12.390
and then as Assistant

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:14.793
Director of the Bush, then Zigler, Center.

00:28:15.694 --> 00:28:16.795
My role at the Zigler

00:28:16.795 --> 00:28:17.962
Center was running the Zigler

00:28:17.962 --> 00:28:19.030
Fellows Program,

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:20.532
which trained Yale affiliates

00:28:20.532 --> 00:28:21.766
at multiple levels

00:28:21.766 --> 00:28:23.034
– undergrads, grad

00:28:23.034 --> 00:28:25.837
students, postdocs, early-career

00:28:25.837 --> 00:28:27.706
and mid-career – on how to work

00:28:27.706 --> 00:28:28.973
at the intersection of child

00:28:28.973 --> 00:28:30.775
development and social policy,

00:28:30.775 --> 00:28:32.544
a field that Ed essentially founded.

00:28:33.645 --> 00:28:35.580
I chose to attend graduate school at Yale

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:37.449
to be part of this fellowship program,

00:28:37.449 --> 00:28:38.650
so it was really incredible

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:41.219
to eventually lead it with Ed.

00:28:41.219 --> 00:28:43.488
We organized a weekly social policy lunch

00:28:43.488 --> 00:28:44.589
lecture series,

00:28:44.589 --> 00:28:46.291
which brought renowned people in the field

00:28:46.291 --> 00:28:48.393
to Yale to speak about their work.

00:28:48.393 --> 00:28:49.861
This was both a Yale course

00:28:49.861 --> 00:28:51.763
and a series open to the entire Yale

00:28:51.763 --> 00:28:52.797
community,

00:28:52.797 --> 00:28:54.966
as well as to the general public in New Haven.

00:28:55.567 --> 00:28:56.701
It was one of the first,

00:28:56.701 --> 00:28:59.137
if not the first, Yale courses of this kind.

00:28:59.738 --> 00:29:01.506
The series drew a large, diverse

00:29:01.506 --> 00:29:02.407
crowd every week

00:29:02.407 --> 00:29:04.476
during the academic year for decades.

00:29:05.477 --> 00:29:06.644
The Zigler Fellows Program

00:29:06.644 --> 00:29:08.146
also featured an annual trip

00:29:08.146 --> 00:29:09.614
to Washington, D.C.,

00:29:09.614 --> 00:29:11.449
where Fellows visited various players

00:29:11.449 --> 00:29:13.651
in the social policymaking process

00:29:13.651 --> 00:29:15.820
on their home turf to learn about their jobs.

00:29:16.387 --> 00:29:18.022
We also had a small seminar

00:29:18.022 --> 00:29:19.290
that Ed and I co-taught.

00:29:20.425 --> 00:29:22.260
Over the course of just the 12 years

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:23.862
I worked at the Zigler Center,

00:29:23.862 --> 00:29:24.796
hundreds of fellows

00:29:24.796 --> 00:29:27.332
participated in the fellowship program.

00:29:27.332 --> 00:29:29.701
Ed, as we all know, had an incredibly long

00:29:29.701 --> 00:29:31.002
list of accomplishments.

00:29:31.002 --> 00:29:32.036
But he always said that

00:29:32.036 --> 00:29:33.271
his proudest accomplishment

00:29:33.271 --> 00:29:34.839
was the many students and fellows

00:29:34.839 --> 00:29:36.941
he trained over the course of his long career.

00:29:37.408 --> 00:29:41.012
There are over 500 Zigler Center fellows

00:29:41.012 --> 00:29:42.113
out there in the world

00:29:42.113 --> 00:29:44.516
just from this one site here alone.

00:29:44.516 --> 00:29:46.084
And they're in academia

00:29:46.084 --> 00:29:47.452
and they're in government

00:29:47.452 --> 00:29:49.020
and they're in philanthropy

00:29:49.020 --> 00:29:51.055
and they're at nonprofit institutes,

00:29:51.055 --> 00:29:52.657
and they're writing about child

00:29:52.657 --> 00:29:55.160
development and social policy for newspapers.

00:29:55.193 --> 00:29:57.328
They are all over the place

00:29:57.328 --> 00:30:00.064
doing all kinds of different elements of work

00:30:00.331 --> 00:30:01.499
necessary in order

00:30:01.499 --> 00:30:03.268
for us to be able to not just talk

00:30:03.268 --> 00:30:05.537
about how to help children and families,

00:30:05.537 --> 00:30:07.105
but actually do it.

00:30:07.105 --> 00:30:08.273
In my current position

00:30:08.273 --> 00:30:09.574
as Chief Research Officer

00:30:09.574 --> 00:30:11.910
at a national child advocacy organization,

00:30:12.177 --> 00:30:13.344
I draw on what I learned

00:30:13.344 --> 00:30:15.180
from Ed on a weekly basis,

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:17.482
and I'm sure that most of Ed's former students

00:30:17.482 --> 00:30:19.784
and Zigler fellows do the same.

00:30:19.784 --> 00:30:21.452
The Zigler Fellow alumni list

00:30:21.452 --> 00:30:22.487
reads like a Who's Who

00:30:22.487 --> 00:30:24.022
of the field of child development

00:30:24.022 --> 00:30:25.557
and social policy.

00:30:25.557 --> 00:30:27.325
Ed was right to be proud,

00:30:27.325 --> 00:30:29.227
and I'm honored to be part of it,

00:30:29.227 --> 00:30:32.630
and among those Ed brought into the field.

00:30:32.630 --> 00:30:33.631
I knew from the beginning

00:30:33.631 --> 00:30:35.233
that I was extremely fortunate

00:30:35.233 --> 00:30:37.302
to have Ed as my mentor,

00:30:37.302 --> 00:30:39.637
but I truly appreciated him

00:30:39.671 --> 00:30:41.306
when I did a summer internship

00:30:41.306 --> 00:30:42.774
at the Office of Child Development,

00:30:42.774 --> 00:30:44.375
which he had led.

00:30:44.475 --> 00:30:47.612
The first thing I saw when I walked in the door

00:30:47.612 --> 00:30:50.215
was this giant portrait of Ed Zigler.

00:30:51.049 --> 00:30:54.752
People there spoke of him with such reverence.

00:30:55.253 --> 00:30:58.056
And over the years, I became increasingly aware

00:30:58.056 --> 00:30:59.791
of the admiration and appreciation

00:30:59.791 --> 00:31:01.426
people had for Ed.

00:31:01.426 --> 00:31:03.494
Everywhere, but especially among child

00:31:03.494 --> 00:31:04.896
advocates and practitioners.

00:31:06.097 --> 00:31:07.665
Ed was a scholar, and he

00:31:07.665 --> 00:31:10.068
advised his students to be scholars first

00:31:10.068 --> 00:31:12.604
if they wanted to have an impact; to earn,

00:31:12.904 --> 00:31:15.607
not to expect, attention from policymakers.

00:31:16.574 --> 00:31:19.177
But at heart, he was an advocate for children,

00:31:19.677 --> 00:31:20.845
especially children

00:31:20.845 --> 00:31:23.815
who are overlooked and underserved.

00:31:23.815 --> 00:31:25.817
That heartfelt commitment to improving

00:31:25.817 --> 00:31:27.085
children's lives

00:31:27.085 --> 00:31:30.421
is what people experienced and revered him for.

00:31:31.189 --> 00:31:32.891
Ed was also savvy

00:31:32.891 --> 00:31:34.893
and extraordinarily effective.

00:31:34.893 --> 00:31:36.527
He was willing to collaborate with people

00:31:36.527 --> 00:31:37.929
from every political side

00:31:37.929 --> 00:31:39.764
to find common ground.

00:31:39.764 --> 00:31:41.032
This is, as we all know, a

00:31:41.032 --> 00:31:43.134
rare quality these days.

00:31:43.134 --> 00:31:44.502
He persisted and regrouped

00:31:44.502 --> 00:31:45.303
when necessary,

00:31:45.303 --> 00:31:46.738
reminding us all

00:31:46.738 --> 00:31:48.139
that we might not be successful

00:31:48.139 --> 00:31:49.474
in a particular moment,

00:31:49.474 --> 00:31:50.642
but we needed to be ready

00:31:50.642 --> 00:31:52.877
when the right moment came along.

00:31:52.877 --> 00:31:54.545
Ed taught us to remain vigilant

00:31:54.545 --> 00:31:55.346
after victories,

00:31:55.346 --> 00:31:57.949
because successes can be easily reversed.

00:31:57.949 --> 00:32:00.218
He had a vision that his students

00:32:00.585 --> 00:32:02.720
and everybody who worked with him

00:32:03.521 --> 00:32:05.990
needed to be the very, very best they could be,

00:32:06.557 --> 00:32:09.294
and that it was his job to inspire

00:32:09.294 --> 00:32:10.628
that bestness

00:32:10.628 --> 00:32:11.963
in all of us.

00:32:11.963 --> 00:32:13.665
If I was working on a chapter

00:32:13.665 --> 00:32:15.800
or working on a journal article with Ed,

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:17.502
I felt like the weight

00:32:17.502 --> 00:32:19.504
of all the children of the world

00:32:19.604 --> 00:32:22.473
was on my shoulders with this article,

00:32:22.774 --> 00:32:25.710
that the lives of babies were in the balance

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:26.911
on the basis of

00:32:26.911 --> 00:32:29.280
just some article that I was writing.

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:30.381
And I think it was really

00:32:30.381 --> 00:32:32.116
because he carried

00:32:32.116 --> 00:32:34.052
that kind of sense of urgency, too.

00:32:34.052 --> 00:32:36.788
If you called him up, you better be prepared

00:32:37.021 --> 00:32:37.889
for the answer

00:32:37.889 --> 00:32:38.756
because he was going

00:32:38.756 --> 00:32:39.891
to give it to you straight.

00:32:39.891 --> 00:32:42.593
And I appreciated that about him.

00:32:42.961 --> 00:32:45.330
If I was off on something,

00:32:45.330 --> 00:32:46.798
he didn't hesitate to tell me.

00:32:46.798 --> 00:32:48.900
He would edit with this big red pen

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:50.034
that you would get it back

00:32:50.034 --> 00:32:51.002
and you would think, Oh my gosh,

00:32:51.002 --> 00:32:53.404
am I going to possibly survive this?

00:32:53.871 --> 00:32:56.708
My office was in many ways a home

00:32:56.708 --> 00:32:57.675
to many students

00:32:57.675 --> 00:32:59.844
after their encounters with Ed.

00:33:00.278 --> 00:33:02.981
So, yes, it is true he was hard, but

00:33:02.981 --> 00:33:05.883
it was always in everybody's best interests

00:33:06.017 --> 00:33:07.618
and we all strive to live up

00:33:07.618 --> 00:33:10.655
to his expectations for all of us.

00:33:10.655 --> 00:33:11.422
And frankly,

00:33:11.422 --> 00:33:14.459
I think he made every one of us better scholars

00:33:15.126 --> 00:33:16.728
as a result of that.

00:33:16.728 --> 00:33:18.529
Ed turned me into a scholar,

00:33:18.529 --> 00:33:19.897
first and foremost,

00:33:19.897 --> 00:33:22.533
whose activism drew from science.

00:33:23.668 --> 00:33:26.070
So, what was it like being Ed student?

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:28.006
It could be terrifying at times.

00:33:28.006 --> 00:33:30.074
He did not tolerate fools

00:33:30.074 --> 00:33:30.641
or students

00:33:30.641 --> 00:33:32.477
who gave up on themselves,

00:33:32.477 --> 00:33:34.712
but he always had his students’ backs.

00:33:35.013 --> 00:33:37.181
He always picked us up when we faltered,

00:33:37.382 --> 00:33:40.018
and he always celebrated our achievements.

00:33:40.318 --> 00:33:42.320
He was like a second father to me.

00:33:42.954 --> 00:33:45.823
Ed is the kind of person who will tell you

00:33:45.823 --> 00:33:47.458
what he thinks to your face

00:33:47.458 --> 00:33:49.560
and defend you behind your back.

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:50.294
If you didn't know

00:33:50.294 --> 00:33:51.529
where you stood with Ed Zigler,

00:33:51.529 --> 00:33:53.731
you simply were not paying attention.

00:33:53.731 --> 00:33:55.933
He would made it perfectly clear

00:33:55.933 --> 00:33:57.335
where things stood.

00:33:57.335 --> 00:33:59.971
But you also knew exactly why he was that way,

00:34:00.238 --> 00:34:02.540
because he took it so seriously.

00:34:02.907 --> 00:34:04.008
And as a result,

00:34:04.008 --> 00:34:06.844
it was impossible to not take it seriously

00:34:06.844 --> 00:34:07.745
with him.

00:34:07.745 --> 00:34:11.215
I remember Larry Aber telling me before I even

00:34:11.482 --> 00:34:12.950
applied to Yale

00:34:13.951 --> 00:34:15.153
that working with

00:34:15.153 --> 00:34:18.589
Ed would give me a tough, exacting,

00:34:19.323 --> 00:34:22.360
irascible adviser,

00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:25.296
but one who would remain steadfast

00:34:25.430 --> 00:34:28.466
and loyal to me for the rest of my career.

00:34:28.633 --> 00:34:31.169
And Larry was so right.

00:34:31.903 --> 00:34:34.172
Ed was with me at every step,

00:34:34.439 --> 00:34:37.875
writing letters of recommendation,

00:34:37.875 --> 00:34:39.377
being a cheerleader,

00:34:39.377 --> 00:34:41.479
telling me I could do it,

00:34:41.479 --> 00:34:43.414
helping me to reflect on things,

00:34:43.414 --> 00:34:46.317
and pushing me to take the next step.

00:34:47.418 --> 00:34:49.454
In my second year of graduate school,

00:34:50.221 --> 00:34:53.257
a family tragedy befell my extended family,

00:34:54.025 --> 00:34:56.327
and I thought I would need to

00:34:56.461 --> 00:34:59.831
drop out of graduate school to fulfill

00:34:59.831 --> 00:35:02.200
my responsibilities to my extended family.

00:35:03.468 --> 00:35:04.836
Ed asked, “Larry,

00:35:04.836 --> 00:35:06.370
do you want to drop out of graduate school?”

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:07.939
And I said, “no.”

00:35:07.939 --> 00:35:10.208
He said, ”then let’s find a way.”

00:35:10.208 --> 00:35:12.243
And he promptly brainstormed with me

00:35:13.444 --> 00:35:15.480
how to keep me in graduate school.

00:35:15.780 --> 00:35:17.849
For me, remembering Ed

00:35:17.849 --> 00:35:19.884
now is more about the ways

00:35:19.884 --> 00:35:21.953
in which he welcomed, encouraged

00:35:22.253 --> 00:35:23.387
and supported me

00:35:23.387 --> 00:35:25.556
and all of his other graduate students

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:27.792
into the world of what I will call

00:35:27.792 --> 00:35:29.760
provocative scholarship,

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:32.830
inextricably linked with impassioned action.

00:35:33.664 --> 00:35:35.433
The admiration and respect that APA

00:35:35.433 --> 00:35:37.034
and the psychology community have for Ed

00:35:37.034 --> 00:35:38.603
are beyond measure.

00:35:38.603 --> 00:35:38.936
Dr.

00:35:38.936 --> 00:35:40.171
Robert Sternberg,

00:35:40.171 --> 00:35:41.239
another former APA

00:35:41.239 --> 00:35:43.341
President, awarded a Presidential Citation

00:35:43.341 --> 00:35:45.243
to Ed in 2003.

00:35:45.243 --> 00:35:46.210
That same year, Ed

00:35:46.210 --> 00:35:47.812
was recognized with a Mentor Award

00:35:47.812 --> 00:35:49.547
in Developmental Psychology,

00:35:49.547 --> 00:35:51.415
and he previously received the APA Award

00:35:51.415 --> 00:35:52.783
for Distinguished Contributions

00:35:52.783 --> 00:35:54.986
to Theory and Research in Community Psychology.

00:35:55.820 --> 00:35:56.888
Yet I understand that Ed

00:35:56.888 --> 00:35:59.190
was the most proud of receiving APA’s

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:00.391
premier award

00:36:00.391 --> 00:36:01.225
for Outstanding

00:36:01.225 --> 00:36:04.128
Lifetime Contributions to Psychology in 2008.

00:36:04.562 --> 00:36:05.696
I will never, ever

00:36:05.696 --> 00:36:07.465
forget my first meeting with Dr.

00:36:07.465 --> 00:36:08.833
Zigler when we were talking to him

00:36:08.833 --> 00:36:11.536
about the idea that grew into Jumpstart,

00:36:11.536 --> 00:36:12.570
the AmeriCorps program

00:36:12.570 --> 00:36:14.372
that trains college students to work one-on-one

00:36:14.372 --> 00:36:15.673
with preschoolers.

00:36:15.673 --> 00:36:16.140
Of course,

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:17.742
we thought he hated the idea

00:36:17.742 --> 00:36:18.876
and was completely unhappy

00:36:18.876 --> 00:36:20.912
and about to throw us out of his office

00:36:20.912 --> 00:36:22.046
based on his demeanor

00:36:22.046 --> 00:36:23.281
and how he was interacting with us

00:36:23.281 --> 00:36:24.515
while we were talking.

00:36:24.515 --> 00:36:26.617
And then at the end, he said, “I like it,

00:36:26.617 --> 00:36:28.186
and I'll give you $1,000 to help

00:36:28.186 --> 00:36:29.887
get this idea off the ground.”

00:36:29.887 --> 00:36:32.256
It completely changed everything for us.

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:34.292
He didn't just give us the thousand dollars

00:36:34.292 --> 00:36:36.360
form the old Bush Center for Child Development,

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:38.329
he also gave us an office

00:36:38.329 --> 00:36:40.298
right around the corner from his office,

00:36:40.298 --> 00:36:42.466
which was the first Jumpstart office ever.

00:36:42.466 --> 00:36:44.769
What started in that office has now grown

00:36:44.769 --> 00:36:46.771
into truly a national movement.

00:36:46.771 --> 00:36:48.472
Over 60,000

00:36:48.472 --> 00:36:49.607
college students have served

00:36:49.607 --> 00:36:50.975
as Jumpstart Corps members.

00:36:50.975 --> 00:36:51.976
They've worked with over

00:36:51.976 --> 00:36:54.045
120,000 preschoolers

00:36:54.045 --> 00:36:55.713
all across the United States.

00:36:55.713 --> 00:36:57.215
And I'm not sure that any of that

00:36:57.215 --> 00:36:58.549
would have happened without Dr.

00:36:58.549 --> 00:36:59.317
Zigler

00:36:59.317 --> 00:37:00.718
giving us that first thousand

00:37:00.718 --> 00:37:02.186
bucks and believing in us

00:37:02.186 --> 00:37:03.654
and believing in young people,

00:37:03.654 --> 00:37:05.556
which is one of the many amazing things

00:37:05.556 --> 00:37:07.892
about Dr. Zigler's entire career.

00:37:07.892 --> 00:37:11.462
I was very early in my career and participating

00:37:11.462 --> 00:37:12.163
in a conference

00:37:12.163 --> 00:37:14.098
in Burlington, Vermont, in which Ed

00:37:14.098 --> 00:37:16.167
was the keynote, of course, as always.

00:37:16.167 --> 00:37:18.736
And I had some very minor role. And

00:37:20.004 --> 00:37:22.273
at some point during the day

00:37:22.273 --> 00:37:23.908
there was a press conference being held,

00:37:23.908 --> 00:37:25.243
and Ed was going to meet

00:37:25.243 --> 00:37:26.510
with the local reporters

00:37:26.510 --> 00:37:29.447
and invited me to come and participate.

00:37:29.947 --> 00:37:32.717
And I said, “Oh my God, no way.

00:37:32.717 --> 00:37:34.652
I'm too intimidated.

00:37:34.652 --> 00:37:36.721
I'm afraid of the press.

00:37:36.721 --> 00:37:38.256
I'm afraid that I'll be misquoted.”

00:37:38.256 --> 00:37:40.024
He looked me in the eye

00:37:40.024 --> 00:37:42.460
and he said, “Let me tell you something.

00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:45.062
If you want to kind of play in this arena

00:37:45.062 --> 00:37:46.964
and want to have an influence,

00:37:46.964 --> 00:37:49.433
you have to put yourself out there

00:37:49.433 --> 00:37:51.736
and be willing to speak to the press

00:37:51.736 --> 00:37:54.805
and do your best at putting out there

00:37:54.805 --> 00:37:55.773
what you want to say.

00:37:55.773 --> 00:37:56.941
But remember,

00:37:56.941 --> 00:37:59.910
never forget that once you say something,

00:38:00.344 --> 00:38:01.679
you have no control over

00:38:01.679 --> 00:38:03.347
what people do with it.

00:38:03.347 --> 00:38:05.316
So you give it your best shot.

00:38:05.316 --> 00:38:07.151
You don't expect to have any say

00:38:07.151 --> 00:38:09.086
about what happens after that.

00:38:09.086 --> 00:38:11.856
And if you can't handle that kind of pressure,

00:38:11.856 --> 00:38:13.357
then don't expect

00:38:13.357 --> 00:38:14.091
that you're going to be able

00:38:14.091 --> 00:38:16.527
to make a difference in that arena.”

00:38:16.694 --> 00:38:18.362
But I never remember Ed ever wanting

00:38:18.362 --> 00:38:19.297
to be on the camera,

00:38:19.297 --> 00:38:21.098
wanting to be covered, wanting to be quoted,

00:38:21.098 --> 00:38:22.767
except if he thought it would assist

00:38:22.767 --> 00:38:24.001
and help the issue.

00:38:24.001 --> 00:38:25.536
Ed embodied for me

00:38:25.536 --> 00:38:28.039
how one can utilize academic training

00:38:28.039 --> 00:38:29.206
to continuously try

00:38:29.206 --> 00:38:31.375
to make the world a better place

00:38:31.375 --> 00:38:32.710
for everyone.

00:38:33.477 --> 00:38:35.880
I look back on my career post-Yale

00:38:35.880 --> 00:38:38.416
and recognize that it was not by accident

00:38:38.416 --> 00:38:40.217
that those 35 some odd years

00:38:40.217 --> 00:38:41.886
at the Hartford Foundation for Public

00:38:41.886 --> 00:38:43.354
Giving were centered around

00:38:43.354 --> 00:38:45.723
the development of policies and programs

00:38:45.723 --> 00:38:48.259
to support young children and their families.

00:38:48.959 --> 00:38:51.762
Moreover, on a very personal level,

00:38:51.762 --> 00:38:53.631
my career as a father

00:38:53.631 --> 00:38:56.701
has been shaped by his teachings, questioning,

00:38:56.701 --> 00:38:59.170
and encouragement to deeply understand

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:02.807
and engage in this remarkable life event.

00:39:02.807 --> 00:39:04.809
Thank you so very much, Ed,

00:39:04.809 --> 00:39:06.610
for helping to steer me onto these

00:39:06.610 --> 00:39:08.379
incredibly important paths

00:39:08.379 --> 00:39:11.215
of learning, of being and of doing.

00:39:12.350 --> 00:39:13.684
It's a real honor and pleasure

00:39:13.684 --> 00:39:14.852
to have the opportunity

00:39:14.852 --> 00:39:17.555
to highlight Ed’s impact on my life.

00:39:17.755 --> 00:39:20.791
I was honored to bring together 23 scholars,

00:39:20.791 --> 00:39:23.861
practitioners and advocates to craft an article

00:39:23.861 --> 00:39:25.896
to honor Ed, titled “Pathways

00:39:25.896 --> 00:39:28.099
to a More Peaceful and Sustainable World.”

00:39:28.933 --> 00:39:31.635
The article provides an overview of selected,

00:39:31.635 --> 00:39:33.337
ongoing international efforts

00:39:33.337 --> 00:39:35.473
that have been inspired by Ed's vision

00:39:36.006 --> 00:39:37.241
to improve programs

00:39:37.241 --> 00:39:39.710
and policies for young children and families.

00:39:40.678 --> 00:39:42.046
The efforts presented are

00:39:42.046 --> 00:39:43.080
in close alignment

00:39:43.080 --> 00:39:45.182
with the strategies articulated by Ed.

00:39:45.850 --> 00:39:47.918
Several  years later, we found out

00:39:47.918 --> 00:39:49.754
 that Ed was going to need surgery.

00:39:49.754 --> 00:39:52.923
And Dr. Kim, the anesthesiologist, went over to Dr.

00:39:52.923 --> 00:39:55.326
Zigler and said, “I was a Head Start graduate.

00:39:55.760 --> 00:39:58.129
My mother said that the only reason

00:39:58.129 --> 00:40:01.399
that I turned out as well off as I did

00:40:01.399 --> 00:40:02.700
was because I had such a good start

00:40:02.700 --> 00:40:03.601
to education

00:40:03.601 --> 00:40:05.703
from the Head Start experience that I had.”

00:40:05.703 --> 00:40:08.372
After the surgery was over, it really wasn't

00:40:08.372 --> 00:40:09.673
the kind of follow up

00:40:09.673 --> 00:40:11.175
that sounded like a doctor

00:40:11.175 --> 00:40:12.376
checking in on a patient.

00:40:12.376 --> 00:40:14.345
It was more like a man checking in

00:40:14.345 --> 00:40:17.248
on his previously unknown benefactor,

00:40:17.248 --> 00:40:18.616
somebody that he felt

00:40:18.616 --> 00:40:21.285
had really helped change his life.

00:40:25.689 --> 00:40:27.691
The numbers people,

00:40:28.459 --> 00:40:31.896
the numbers of policies

00:40:33.197 --> 00:40:34.765
and the numbers

00:40:34.765 --> 00:40:37.001
of visionary steps

00:40:39.069 --> 00:40:42.506
that he architected

00:40:42.506 --> 00:40:45.876
led this nation to a far better,

00:40:46.243 --> 00:40:48.979
however still imperfect but

00:40:49.246 --> 00:40:52.850
far better, agenda for children and families.

00:40:53.451 --> 00:40:54.285
He was brilliant.

00:40:54.285 --> 00:40:56.487
He was at the cutting edge of everything

00:40:56.487 --> 00:40:58.489
and a visionary in every way.

00:41:00.090 --> 00:41:03.127
So, we all have a lot to appreciate

00:41:03.127 --> 00:41:04.929
and be thankful for.

00:41:05.429 --> 00:41:07.832
Fifty years ago, Sargent Shriver,

00:41:07.832 --> 00:41:10.734
one of the architects of the War on Poverty,

00:41:10.734 --> 00:41:13.471
handpicked a brilliant young

00:41:13.471 --> 00:41:17.908
academic from Yale University, Edward Zigler,

00:41:17.908 --> 00:41:19.677
to serve as a leading expert

00:41:19.677 --> 00:41:22.146
tasked with designing the program

00:41:22.146 --> 00:41:24.782
that became Head Start.

00:41:24.782 --> 00:41:27.518
President Johnson spoke of this project as,

00:41:27.518 --> 00:41:31.288
and I quote, “a new war front on poverty.”

00:41:31.722 --> 00:41:32.923
He said,

00:41:32.923 --> 00:41:36.093
and again, quote, “We set out to make certain

00:41:36.627 --> 00:41:39.997
that poverty’s children would not be forever

00:41:39.997 --> 00:41:42.900
more poverty’s captives.”

00:41:43.767 --> 00:41:44.969
Professor Zigler soon

00:41:44.969 --> 00:41:47.204
became known as the “Father of Head Start”

00:41:47.838 --> 00:41:49.740
and with good reason.

00:41:49.740 --> 00:41:53.310
And even today, it is difficult to overstate

00:41:53.310 --> 00:41:56.947
how audacious, how revolutionary, Dr.

00:41:56.947 --> 00:41:59.283
Zigler's vision really is.

00:41:59.283 --> 00:42:00.584
Head Start brings together

00:42:00.584 --> 00:42:02.820
everything, everything

00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:03.821
that matters most

00:42:03.821 --> 00:42:07.258
to young children: education, health,

00:42:07.591 --> 00:42:10.394
emotional well-being, nutrition,

00:42:10.661 --> 00:42:12.763
social services.

00:42:12.763 --> 00:42:15.599
Instead of treating children as isolated

00:42:15.599 --> 00:42:19.703
individuals, Head Start embraces whole families

00:42:20.070 --> 00:42:21.171
involving parents

00:42:21.171 --> 00:42:24.341
and making sure their kids are ready to learn.

00:42:25.276 --> 00:42:26.777
Head Start is without question,

00:42:26.777 --> 00:42:28.345
the most effective early

00:42:28.345 --> 00:42:30.114
childhood development program

00:42:30.114 --> 00:42:34.952
that our country has ever developed.

00:42:35.553 --> 00:42:38.155
His gifts to the United States

00:42:38.155 --> 00:42:40.558
do not stop with Head Start.

00:42:41.492 --> 00:42:43.127
He helped give our country, family

00:42:43.127 --> 00:42:46.430
and medical leave, assistance with child care,

00:42:47.131 --> 00:42:49.500
Zero to Three.

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:52.269
And, with Dr. Jim Comer, the CoZi Initiative,

00:42:52.803 --> 00:42:55.406
the Yale Child Study Center.

00:42:55.406 --> 00:42:58.008
But his greatest legacy

00:42:58.008 --> 00:43:00.277
is the stars

00:43:00.511 --> 00:43:02.513
too numerous to name

00:43:02.513 --> 00:43:04.982
who have been trained at Yale

00:43:04.982 --> 00:43:06.750
under his tutelage.

00:43:06.750 --> 00:43:08.319
Ed left an indelible mark

00:43:08.319 --> 00:43:10.321
 on our community and our nation.

00:43:10.921 --> 00:43:12.456
It was my great privilege

00:43:12.456 --> 00:43:14.525
to not only have had the opportunity

00:43:14.525 --> 00:43:16.160
to work closely with him,

00:43:16.160 --> 00:43:18.362
but to have called him my friend.

00:43:18.362 --> 00:43:21.365
And today, it is my great honor to join

00:43:21.365 --> 00:43:22.967
all of you in remembering

00:43:22.967 --> 00:43:25.269
Ed’s invaluable contributions

00:43:25.269 --> 00:43:27.504
not only to his chosen profession,

00:43:27.504 --> 00:43:29.773
but to all of our lives.

00:43:29.773 --> 00:43:32.009
May his spirit and vision continue

00:43:32.343 --> 00:43:34.411
to be a guide to us all.

00:43:34.845 --> 00:43:35.512
When I'm asked

00:43:35.512 --> 00:43:37.147
what am I most proud of

00:43:37.147 --> 00:43:38.349
as a senator and author

00:43:38.349 --> 00:43:40.117
of a lot of legislation,

00:43:40.117 --> 00:43:42.519
I come back to children every single time.

00:43:43.020 --> 00:43:44.421
Other things may have had a

00:43:44.421 --> 00:43:46.657
larger impact at the moment and so forth.

00:43:46.957 --> 00:43:50.160
But nothing I did will have the lasting impact

00:43:50.494 --> 00:43:52.062
that the cluster of issues

00:43:52.062 --> 00:43:53.764
around children and their families did, 

00:43:53.764 --> 00:43:55.299
and Ed Ziglr was the author

00:43:55.299 --> 00:43:56.967
of an awful lot of those ideas

00:43:56.967 --> 00:43:58.469
and how to get them done.

00:43:58.469 --> 00:44:00.437
He wanted us to always do better.

00:44:00.437 --> 00:44:02.039
He wanted our country to do better.

00:44:02.039 --> 00:44:05.676
He wanted scholars to do better by our country.

00:44:06.076 --> 00:44:07.044
He wanted those of us

00:44:07.044 --> 00:44:08.045
who were in the profession

00:44:08.045 --> 00:44:09.446
of teachers and school administrators,

00:44:09.446 --> 00:44:10.948
he wanted us to do better

00:44:10.948 --> 00:44:12.416
because he knew we could.

00:44:12.416 --> 00:44:13.017
And he knew

00:44:13.017 --> 00:44:14.985
there was a better future for children,

00:44:14.985 --> 00:44:17.121
if only we dedicated ourselves

00:44:17.121 --> 00:44:18.922
more fully to it.

00:44:18.922 --> 00:44:22.159
Upon receiving one of his numerous awards, Ed

00:44:22.159 --> 00:44:24.061
reflected on the Head Start program

00:44:24.061 --> 00:44:25.529
he conceptualized.

00:44:25.529 --> 00:44:26.463
He said,

00:44:26.630 --> 00:44:29.466
“If I had never done anything else in my life,

00:44:30.034 --> 00:44:32.469
that would have been a life worth living.”

00:44:33.437 --> 00:44:34.972
But of course, Head Start was

00:44:34.972 --> 00:44:37.074
just one way Ed improved our world.

00:44:38.075 --> 00:44:41.011
He advised every US president from Lyndon

00:44:41.011 --> 00:44:42.980
Johnson to Barack Obama

00:44:42.980 --> 00:44:45.482
on child and family policy.

00:44:45.482 --> 00:44:46.950
He was a prolific scholar

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:50.087
who published over 40 books and 800 papers,

00:44:51.088 --> 00:44:53.190
and he was pivotal to the Family

00:44:53.190 --> 00:44:55.259
and Medical Leave Act’s passage.

00:44:56.193 --> 00:44:57.428
Throughout his life,

00:44:57.428 --> 00:44:59.830
Ed brought much honor to Yale,

00:44:59.830 --> 00:45:02.833
and an equal measure of hope to millions

00:45:02.833 --> 00:45:05.903
of youths across America.

00:45:05.903 --> 00:45:08.906
As we pay tribute to our devoted colleague,

00:45:09.606 --> 00:45:11.275
let us find solace in the fact

00:45:11.275 --> 00:45:13.777
that Ed Zigler's legacy endures

00:45:14.078 --> 00:45:16.613
through the many lives he enriched.

